      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm 2).$",
